Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
in the middle of finals week several  universities across the u.s are going  virtual  and it's all because of concern over  rising infections cornell university is  the latest school to send students home  after more than 900 of them tested  positive for covid over the last week  this comes despite cornell's high  vaccination rate more than 98 percent of  its students are fully inoculated so  joining us now to talk about the fight  against kovid on campus is michael  kotlikoff he is cornell university  provost  uh provost kotlikov thank you so much  for joining us on quick take if you  could start us off by just walking us  through your thinking on pivoting to  virtual learning right at the end of the  semester was there a threshold or a  cutoff number that raised enough  eyebrows for the school to say okay this  is when we go virtual  sure well i think it goes back to  thanksgiving when we saw students return  we had  the somewhat expected and usual spike in  cases  however that spike kept going up and we  were increasing at uh roughly 50 percent  of  cases every every day  and that alarmed us and we began to see  a pattern of infection that was  different than what we had seen before  we had prepared with a test that's a  proxy test for  the omicron variant and so we analyzed a  number of the samples and we saw a very  high  percentage  of these cases were  associated with this s gene dropout or  the proxy for omicron so that told us we  were dealing with something different  and we needed to do something different  to stop the raws  what is it looking like on campus though  are you seeing more and more students  getting super sick has anyone gotten  hospitalized so far  or so far is it infection rather than  illness  yeah great question  no one on cam no student has been  hospitalized at cornell since the  beginning of the fall semester so it's  all  primarily mild disease or asymptomatic  disease but of course  if if it spreads and it spreads  throughout the the student population  exponentially we want to control that so  it doesn't get  affects so many students  that the chances of serious disease go  up  so what does that mean in terms of  normal university operations campus  facilities are closed and finals that  are normally taken in person have been  moved online um but are students allowed  to still gather or do they need to  socially distance what does it look like  on campus in terms of what people are  allowed to do and where they're allowed  to go  yeah we we um as you say pivoted to  online for our students the dorms are  open or  our um dining halls are open although  we're doing more grab-and-go food to try  and decrease the crowding there's social  distancing mask wearing  we're  allowing students of course to leave if  they've been  tested within the last 48 hours so we  know that it's safe for them to travel  home  but the university is operating  offices are open the  our laboratories are open this has  mainly been an infection in our student  body  and  that's what we wanted to make sure that  any student gatherings any activities  that involve particularly undergraduate  students  we stopped and made sure that we ended  the semester safely  what other big health restrictions are  you going to put in to keep students  healthy is there going to be a booster  shot requirement coming up and if so how  are you going to plan to monitor that  particularly since everyone can get  their booster at different times and i  assume you also have a lot of  international students who may not have  access to boosters if they're going home  for the holidays so what will that  requirement look like if you put that in  well we've started already  we've urged our students to get  boosters  those that are eligible that are six  months past their their last  vaccination  we've had several clinics on campus  we've provided information about where  to locally get vaccinations  as we begin to think about the  the beginning of the spring semester  we cornell starts as you know a little  bit later than uh other universities so  at the end of january  we'll make the decision about boosters  in the next several days and be  communicating that soon  and that is the we'll make the decision  about mandating boosters uh over the  next several days gotcha but at the same  time you are encouraging students to get  boosters if they do qualify and and  maddie brings up a good point it's  something she and i have talked about  which is that people tend to get a  little bit reluctant maybe even lazy and  complacent about getting their boosters  because  it takes time out of your day you're  already inoculated you might have a  negative reaction and who has time for  that particularly before the holidays  is there anything that you can do the  school can do to incentivize students to  get their booster shots if they qualify  well i think the biggest incentive is a  mandate and and it's something we're  very seriously considering  any mandate would need to be a rolling  mandate because many of our students had  their second vaccine  in august or september  so they won't be eligible until the  well into the spring semester  but what we're really trying to do is  make sure we get as immune and resistant  a population  as possible and of course  you know the the  sharpest tool in our toolbox really the  most effective tool is getting people  immunized and then boosted and there's  now emerging evidence that that's  protective against the omicron variant  which has spread so rapidly on our  campus there is and i hate to be kind of  a negative nancy here but unfortunately  breakthrough cases are just increasingly  common  i even among people who are double  inoculated triple inoculated with  boosters so at what point do you  envision the school kind of accepting  this as the norm just like the flu  students are going to get sick no matter  what so when do you think you'll make  that transition to sort of an  acceptance that this is just par for the  course  yeah it's a very good question right now  we as i said we've had very little  disease on campus  but  recently a substantial amount of  infectivity i think we need a little bit  more information about the pathogenicity  the the seriousness of disease  associated with the omicron variant we  know a lot more about the delta variant  if as  initial reports are saying  the the omicron variant is less  pathogenic  then i think we're will that we'll call  the question that you're really asking  and we'll  have to make a decision about whether we  can live with this virus and  and that also i think depends on our  ability to treat individuals that get  sick and the other thing of course is  that more of those treatments are coming  out all the time so we're we've been i  think from the beginning  making our decisions based on the  science and the and the public health of  our community  so spring semester you mentioned starts  in late january people get to go home  the students will be able to go home for  a couple of weeks when they come back  omicron could still be raging  that's plenty of time for another  variant to emerge as well and who knows  what that one might look like  i know that no one wants to go back to  the days of march 2020 where we  basically shut down and all the students  went home but  is there a plan in place for if that  were to happen or if that needed to  happen have you guys drawn up plans for  what would be required for the school to  shut down once again and close  everything  well right now we're working on our our  spring uh plans and you know the first  thing that we really have to understand  is  whether it's safe  to conduct classes  have students in classrooms  of course masked in those classrooms and  vaccinated and immune  but safe and and not a risk of  transmission we we've done a lot of the  initial modeling around a coveted 19  transmission  and we have a team on campus that's  doing that modeling feeding in the new  data about infectivity of the omicron  variant  and  so our first effort really over the next  couple of weeks will be to understand  the biology of this virus  and then make the determination  whether we need to de-densify our  classes whether it's safe to have all  our students in class  whether we need to go hybrid  all of those decisions will be made in  the coming weeks well speaking of if i'm  a student who's planning to go home for  the holidays i may hear this and think  okay i'm not going to be in the  classroom i may not be able to go to  parties what's the point in going back  next semester maybe i stay at home and  save a little bit of money what would  your message be to those students who  are going through that decision as they  hear about uh going virtual  yeah we we've tried very very hard to  optimize the student experience from the  beginning of this pandemic so you may  know cornell um in in the fall of 2020  had all of our students come back into  our residence halls we conducted a safe  semester we had many of our classes in  person  these uh the spring semester last spring  semester and this fall semester we've  been at at full density and and had our  our  our students in class  safely  again no disease  so um i i really think that the  residential campus experience for  undergraduates particularly  is as a precious time and we're doing  everything that we can to make sure that  we optimize those that experience for  those students i'm sure that our cornell  alum here agrees with that yes obviously  there's nothing quite like being in  ithaca in january or february  i'm sure the students are itching to get  back michael uh really appreciate you  joining us and breaking all that down  thank you so much i hope you all stay  safe that was cornell university provost  michael kotlikoff joining us

